WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.210
- [Narrator] With hits like Squid Game,

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:05.750
Don't Look Up, Bridgerton,
and Stranger Things,

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:08.293
Netflix has long been
the leader in streaming,

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.350
but in April the company
reported a drop in subscribers

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.370
for the first time in a decade.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.099
- We're getting pretty
high market penetration.

00:00:17.099 --> 00:00:19.400
And that combined with a competition

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.419
is really you know,
what we think is driving

00:00:22.419 --> 00:00:25.240
the lower acquisition and lower growth.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.110
- [Narrator] The decline
is uncharted territory

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.380
for the company which is
facing growing competition

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:30.950
from other streamers.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:32.450
- They are maturing,

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.513
as other competitors are starting to grow.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.680
- [Narrator] Here are three strategies

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:38.830
that Netflix might try

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.040
to break free of their subscriber plateau

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.510
and how these methods have
worked for other streamers.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.350
- Oh, that that's, that's terrific.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.740
- [Narrator] One strategy that
Netflix is already working on

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.230
is reevaluating how it
creates and acquires content.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.370
- We have to have an Adam
Project and a Bridgerton

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:54.550
every month and to make sure

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.150
that that's the expectation
of the service constantly.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.550
- [Narrator] Netflix has a wide library

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.060
that continues to grow.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.830
In 2021, it released more
than 500 original programs

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.670
according to people familiar
with the company strategy.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.170
But it's led to a mixed bag

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.070
of hits and misses for the company.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.300
- Netflix spends upwards
of $20 billion a year

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.940
acquiring programming, making programming.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.900
They don't wanna reduce that amount

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.380
but they would like to
spend it a little better.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.100
- [Narrator] Consistent quality
content has proven important

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.420
for gaining and retaining subscribers.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:29.430
Disney+ and HBO Max
saw subscription spikes

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.800
for big releases like
Hamilton and Wonder Woman '84

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.040
but many of those subscribers
canceled their plans

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.360
within six months.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.120
- You have to strike a balance
of keeping the machine going

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.160
and fresh stuff coming
in without overwhelming

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.280
your customer base.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.710
But yes, what you don't want
of course are large periods

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:52.260
between new shows coming out
and or hit shows returning.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.850
- [Narrator] Another avenue
that Netflix has said

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.150
they will explore is adding
a cheaper ad-supported option

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.350
for subscriptions.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.620
- Netflix has been very
against advertising,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.270
against the idea of advertising,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.040
the co-founder Reed
Hastings has always said

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.390
this is a platform built for the viewers,

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:09.640
built for our subscribers.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:13.210
Our subscribers don't want
ads, ads interrupt the content.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:14.240
- [Narrator] Most other streamers

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.430
already offer ad-supported options.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.380
It's been a staple of
Hulu's plans for years.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.110
Ad-supported subscriptions
make up about 59% of its base.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.540
According data from subscriber
measurement company Antenna.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.060
HBO Max began offering a
9.99 subscription with ads

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.810
in June of 2021 compared
to its regular 14.99

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.280
ad-free version.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.710
After about six months

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.930
ad supported subscriptions
made up about 8% of its base,

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.170
according to Antenna.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:44.180
And Disney+ announced that
they'd release a lower cost

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.660
ad-supported option later this year.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.710
A small, but growing number of subscribers

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:50.960
are choosing ad-supported plans.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.910
According to Antenna,
they accounted for 32%

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.100
of streaming signups in 2021

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.550
compared with only 19% the year before,

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:00.610
which translated into a small increase

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.320
in subscriptions as well.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.180
- Of course, what Netflix doesn't wanna do

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.210
is see a lot of their current subscribers

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.360
who pay a lot of money
to not have commercials

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.320
decide economically it's a better bet

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.940
to deal with some commercials.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.760
So they're gonna have to balance that out,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.210
but the hope is new
revenue from commercials,

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.020
new subscribers coming in.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.290
- [Narrator] Saving money
accounts for about 30%

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.670
of subscription cancellations in the U.S.,

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.820
according to survey data

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.040
from Kantar Entertainment On Demand.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.720
Offering a lower price
might entice more holdouts

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.350
who don't wanna pay Netflix's
current lowest plan.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.560
- People are being very
cost conscious these days.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.160
The idea that you cut the cord

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.627
and save money through streaming
is a little bit of a myth.

00:03:39.627 --> 00:03:41.930
And the more these services go up in price

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.340
the more people will realize
it's a bit of a myth.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.330
- [Narrator] The trickiest strategy

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:46.620
Netflix wants to focus on

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.960
is cracking down on password sharing,

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.870
an issue that streamers have
been trying to solve for years.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.710
- Streamers have a love hate relationship

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.070
with password sharing.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.320
Streamers don't want to be the bad cop

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:00.280
cracking down on people
sharing their passwords

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.450
but at some point it becomes so widespread

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.040
that it affects the bottom line.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.920
- [Narrator] Netflix estimates
that password sharing

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.810
is losing them a large
portion of customers.

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:12.230
222 Million households
subscribe worldwide.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.050
But the company estimates
that an additional

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.360
100 million households access the service

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:17.810
through shared passwords.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.560
- Viewer penetration is
made up of two groups.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.400
One is a group that's
paying us, which is great.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.360
And then there's a group of
viewers that are not paying us

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.240
and they're sharing someone
else's account credential.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.370
And we really see that second group

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.900
is a tremendous opportunity.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:35.500
- [Narrator] Netflix
already has some measures

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.980
to prevent password sharing.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.030
There's a limit on the number of profiles

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.320
an account can have and
different subscription levels

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:44.420
have caps for how many devices
can be watching at a time.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:47.070
Other streamers currently
follow similar models.

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:48.790
A new method Netflix might try

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:51.170
is adding an additional
charge for accounts used

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:52.800
in more than one household.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.490
They're already testing
subscription models

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:57.490
like this in Chile, Peru and Costa Rica.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.290
- There are going to be customers upset.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:01.240
People get used to
getting something for free

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.600
or feel like I subscribe,

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.570
I should be able to share this with my son

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.520
and my daughter who live in another state.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.830
And so they're gonna have to be,

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.120
do a good job in explaining
that the only way

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.930
we can keep bringing you
all the programming you love

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.800
is to have more revenue to make it.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.380
- [Narrator] Whatever
Netflix chooses to do,

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.430
the industry will be watching closely.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.000
- With Netflix is going to
to try to be more efficient

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.050
or to use their words, right
size their programming budgets,

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:30.470
that has a ripple effect on
the rest of the industry.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:31.930
I'm sure for the rest of the industry,

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:32.910
they're thinking, great.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.540
These guys have been overpaying

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.100
and blowing budgets out
of proportion for years.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.150
So now everything may actually return

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:40.990
to some sort of sense of normal.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:42.240
- [Narrator] In addition
to these measures,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.320
Netflix will also likely
turn their attention

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.410
to international markets like Asia

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.777
where streaming is still fairly
new and not yet saturated.

00:05:49.777 --> 00:05:52.527
(dramatic music)

